v1.6.8-pre (unreleased)
- fixed: O(n^2) time in the binary-tree match finder (HC L13-L15, seq
  L12-L15) on a long repetitive run that does not reach the end of the
  input: every tree insert compared the candidate up to the end of the
  buffer, although any length past LZ6_OPT_NUM only triggers a break. The
  comparison now stops at LZ6_OPT_NUM+1 bytes: output byte-identical, and
  1 MB of a 326-byte pattern plus 16 other bytes goes from 27 s to 0.25 s
  (HC L13-L15), 109 s to 0.9 s (seq L15). Reported by zpaq-std. Present
  since the LZ5 lineage (a8e1b51 has it too).
- perf/encode (L1-L2): the fast parser's input-scaled hash table is capped
  at 2^20 entries (was up to 2^23): it is bound by hash-table misses.
  Full Silesia encode +18-20% (L1 81-89 -> 96-106 MB/s, L2 80-83 -> 95-100),
  size L1 33.43% -> 33.68%, L2 31.86% -> 32.03%; AIT unchanged (its files
  are below the cap). L3-L15 byte-identical.
  Measured and not taken: a tiny table plus no lazy matching reaches only
  ~140 MB/s (Silesia subset) at 43% -- the rest of the per-byte cost is
  the multi-pass pipeline (sequence arrays, symbol conversion, entropy
  stages), not the match search. zstd -1-class speed needs a single-pass
  fast encoder.
- perf/seq decode: Huffman literals in 4 interleaved streams (lit_mode 7,
  zstd's HUF 4X) from 4096 literals up: one code table, the literals split
  in 4 equal segments coded as independent MSB-first streams, decoded in
  one loop with each stream's state in registers. Silesia decode cycles
  (one core): L1 -12.5%, L2 -13.6%, L5 -11%, L12 -9.5%; size +12 bytes
  per block. BREAKS THE SEQ BLOCK FORMAT (a new literal mode; blocks with
  fewer literals keep mode 5).
  Two traps on the way, both measured: (1) with the four streams' state in
  arrays indexed by stream, the loop went through memory and was 8-10%
  slower than one stream; (2) the new mode's setup code, inlined into the
  decoder, changed GCC's register allocation of the whole function and
  cost the sequence loop ~12% on identical input -- it now lives in a
  noinline helper and the decoder's state struct did not grow.

v1.6.7-pre
- levels/encode: L3-L5 use a new row-hash match finder (LZ6HC_row, zstd
  1.5-style: rows of 16 positions with 8-bit tags compared at once, SSE2
  or portable SWAR; prefetch 8 positions ahead; rep0-first lazy parser,
  candidates ranked by 4*length - log2(offset)). Same or better ratio than
  the chain parsers at up to 2x the speed. Full Silesia (was):
  L3 29.14% (30.71), L4 28.50% (28.87), L5 28.06% (28.32); AIT L3-L5
  -0.1..-1.5%. Encode, Silesia subset: L3 57, L4 45, L5 38 MB/s (were
  67, 22, 21). The price pre-parse of L8-L15 got its own parameter row,
  so those levels no longer depend on L3 (byte-identical to the L3-L7
  retune below; vs v1.6.6-pre they moved by <= 0.1 point, from the smaller
  decode tables and that retune's pre-parse).
  Table sizing matters: sized to the input (up to 2^24 slots) the rows
  ran at 22 MB/s whatever the search depth, every insert a cache miss;
  capped at 2^22-2^23 slots and prefetched, 45-58 MB/s.
- perf/decode (L4-L7): Huffman now also wins near-ties against the
  order-1 literal coders (within 3%). mozilla at L4-L7 chose 256-context
  order-1 literals for a 1% smaller literal stream and decoded 2.6x
  slower; Silesia L4-L6 decode 10.2-10.6 -> 6.7-7.0 cyc/B. This also
  fixes the L4-L7 decode regression of the L3-L7 retune below (measured
  only for encode speed when it went in). L6/L7 +0.02 points.
- levels: L3-L7 retuned from a sweep of the existing parsers. L3 moves
  from the fast parser to price_fast; L4-L7 use lowest_price with 6-byte
  hashes (was 4) and 1/2/4/8 candidates, and a 2^15 base hash (the
  density-aware widening sizes it for large inputs; the old 2^23 rows
  zeroed 32 MB even for small ones). Every level is smaller and no slower
  than before. Full Silesia L3 31.75% -> 30.71%, L4 30.04 -> 28.87,
  L5 29.26 -> 28.32, L6 28.89 -> 27.90, L7 28.19 -> 27.60; AIT L3-L7
  -0.4..-0.9%. Encode (one core, Silesia subset) L1-L8: 95 88 67 22 21 17
  14 6.2 MB/s.
  L3 is also the optimal levels' price pre-parse: Silesia L8-L12 gain
  ~0.1 point, L15 unchanged (25.45%); AIT L8-L15 lose 0.03-0.05. A
  level-4 pre-parse was about as good but makes L8 ~17% slower.
- fixed (experiment hook only): LZ6_SEQPARAMS with H3=0 on the chain
  strategies shifted a 32-bit hash by 32 and wrote outside hashTable3.
- perf/seq decode: -5% cycles (Silesia L2 8.27 -> 7.8 cyc/B, L12 6.87 ->
  6.53). Sequence decode entries carry the value base and extra-bit count
  (zstd's seqSymbol), so ll/ml/offset need no per-symbol table lookups or
  branches; the two-stage pipeline runs fill / steady A+B / drain with no
  scheduling branch; LL/ML tables default to log 10 so the 8-byte entries
  stay in L1 (+0.02-0.05% size). Format unchanged.
  Tried and rejected: reading all of a sequence's bits at once (-9%
  instructions, +4-6% cycles: a longer dependency chain); a single-check
  fast path in stage B (+7-9%, code size).

v1.6.6-pre
- ratio/seq: offsets keep their low 3 bits in the entropy-coded symbol.
  Offsets 1..31 get one code each; above, the code is the bucket
  (floor(log2)) plus the low 3 bits, and only the bits in between are raw.
  The low bits carry the alignment of structured data (4/8-byte fields,
  record strides), which the old raw residual threw away (LZMA's "align
  bits", here at no extra decoder state). The optimal parser prices new
  offsets per (bucket, low 3 bits), so it prefers aligned ones.
  Offset alphabet 28 -> 194 symbols, table log 11; the tANS table header
  is now varints with zero runs (a 194-symbol table costs ~100-200 bytes).
  BREAKS THE SEQ BLOCK FORMAT (lab format; LZ6S1 fixture regenerated at
  level 9). Full Silesia: L2 32.07% -> 31.85%, L8 27.69% -> 27.39%,
  L12 25.93% -> 25.65%, L15 25.72% -> 25.43% (zstd -19's lead 0.76 ->
  0.47 points); mozilla -3.7%, sao/osdb/nci gain most, text ~0.
  CLI -B1 / -B2 / -B5 on Silesia L12 -1.05 / -1.03 / -1.06%; AIT
  -0.26 / -0.07 / 0.00%; a 96-file mixed corpus sample -0.2..-0.4%.
  Decode +1..3% cycles (+0.5..1.4% instructions, a larger offset
  table); encode unchanged.
  Measured first offline on dumped parses: bucket slots (top bits of the
  residual, as in deflate/LZMA) gained only -0.1%; v1's per-bucket top-8
  model -0.6..1.5% on mozilla/nci; the low bits (joint with the bucket or
  as a separate align state, equal) -2.7% on mozilla and sao.
- perf/seq encode: levels 1-3 ~2.5x faster, 4-7 1.1-1.4x, 8-15 1.03-1.11x
  (one core, Silesia subset, MB/s by level: 106 102 89 27 25 22 17 8.5
  6.0 4.4 4.2 3.4 1.8 1.2 0.9; v1.6.5-pre: 42 41 40 19 19 18 15 7.7 5.5
  4.2 3.8 3.1 1.7 1.2 0.9). Full Silesia L1-3 ~40 -> ~70 MB/s.
  - the byte-plane trial now also needs the lanes to differ (entropy
    spread >= 2.7 b/B). It ran a full second encode on every input with
    one lane under 5 b/B, which includes all text (a novel's lanes sit at
    ~4.4 b/B each), and lost there every time. On Silesia + AIT the plane
    transform wins on exactly the same files as before (F, x-ray, E, G,
    mr; losers sat at spread <= 2.3). The lane entropies are sampled
    (16 x 64KB) instead of four full passes.
  - the density-aware hash of levels 1-7 is 8x smaller (density ~16,
    capped at 2^23): the fast parsers are hash-miss bound. Silesia size
    L1 +0.09pt, L2 +0.07, L3 +0.03, L4-7 ~0. L8-15 move by +-0.04%
    (their price pre-parse is level 3).
  - output-identical: Huffman literal encoder with a 64-bit accumulator
    and 32-bit flushes; the sequence bitstream writer flushes once per
    field group instead of per byte (-8% cycles at L2); literals stay
    bytes unless a rANS coder runs (was 4 B/literal); the fast parser
    prefetches the next probe's hash entry; the fast strategies skip
    zeroing the (unused) chain table.
  Tried and rejected: prefetching the match source one probe ahead (no
  gain); building the block in dst (callers pass dstCap = srcSize-1, so
  the temp block stays).
- fixed: a signed shift overflow (UB) when decoding a corrupt plane block
  header, found by the UBSan fuzz gate.

v1.6.5-pre
- levels: the seq engine gets its own level table (the frame HC codec keeps
  its table and output), retuned as a strictly monotonic ladder: every
  level compresses better and slower than the one before, where the old
  table had byte-identical pairs (L4=L5, L6=L7) and near-duplicates
  (L8, L12, L14). L8-L10 now use the entropy-priced optimal parser with
  cheap searches (the old gap: L10 13.7 MB/s -> L11 3.6 MB/s); L13-L15
  add refinement re-parses (1/2/3), which measured better than deeper
  searches (256-1024 searches gave the same size as 64).
  Full Silesia, level: 1 33.52%, 2 32.00%, 3 32.00%, 4 30.37%, 5 29.59%,
  6 29.21%, 7 28.50%, 8 27.69%, 9 27.40%, 10 26.39%, 11 26.18%,
  12 25.94%, 13 25.78%, 14 25.74%, 15 25.72%.
  Encode (single core, Silesia subset), MB/s: 37 37 33 19 17 16 14 7.1
  5.4 4.3 4.0 3.2 1.6 1.0 0.8. Level 2 (the seq level the README suggests) is
  unchanged; old L15 files decode as always (encoder-only change).
- ratio/seq: the optimal parser (levels 11-15) now prices with what the
  seq coder charges instead of the frame codec's codeword bytes: -log2
  frequency of the ll/ml/offset symbols plus their extra bits, and the
  literals' order-0 entropy (the byte prices charged a literal 8 bits and
  mispriced offsets both ways). The statistics come from a cheap level-3
  pre-parse, freed before the main context is allocated; level 15 adds
  one refinement re-parse. The frame HC codec keeps its byte prices and is
  byte-identical.
  Silesia L15 26.52% -> 25.77% (-2.84%; zstd -19's lead 1.56 -> 0.81
  points), L11 26.76% -> 26.17%; AIT L15 39.54% -> 38.82%. Per file L15:
  dickens -6%, reymont -6%, nci -5%, webster -4.7%, C -6%, B -4%.
  Decode gets faster too (fewer sequences): -8..9% cycles.
  Encode: L11 about the same speed, L15 ~2x slower (the refinement pass).
  Tried and rejected: the same prices in the heuristic parsers of levels
  4-10 (better_price / lowest_price split point): +2..3% larger at L6/L9,
  neutral at L4. Those greedy/lazy parsers decide one sequence at a time,
  and the byte model's 8-bit literal overprices literals in a way that
  compensates for their short view; only the full DP gains from true
  costs.

v1.6.4-pre
- fixed: the fast LZ encoder (-0, and the light-LZ fallback inside seq
  frames) crashed on Windows x86 (32-bit). On 32-bit hosts its hash table
  held pointers whose empty (NULL) entries were rejected only by the
  distance check; lz6's 16 MB window let NULL pass when the heap sits
  below 16 MB, as it does on Windows x86, and the encoder read address 0.
  It now always uses the index table (as 64-bit hosts already did); 64-bit
  output is unchanged. Present since the window grew to 16 MB (v1.6.3-pre
  win32 is affected; Linux x86 was not, its heap is high).
- perf/seq v2: the ll/ml/of symbols move from three rANS streams to
  table ANS (zstd FSE-style: one table load + a bit read per symbol, no
  multiply), and their states share ONE backward bitstream with every
  extra bit (ll/ml extras; offsets as bucket + raw bits). Six sections
  (3 rANS streams, 25 per-bucket offset top-8 streams, low bits, extra
  bits) become three small table headers and one stream; the decoder
  reads a sequence with one refill.
  BREAKS THE SEQ BLOCK FORMAT (lab format, no compatibility shim; the
  LZ6S1 fixture is regenerated). The frame HC levels are untouched.
  Measured (one core, interleaved vs master, Silesia): decode L2
  ~376 -> ~459 MB/s, L15 ~393 -> ~485 MB/s (cycles/byte 9.95 -> 8.29,
  9.26 -> 7.65). Size: Silesia L2 +0.27%, L15 +0.50% (the dropped
  offset model; mozilla/nci/sao/x-ray carry it); AIT L2 -0.06%, L15
  -0.04% (fewer stream headers).
  Tried and rejected: two interleaved sequence bitstreams (+6.5% cycles:
  the decoder is instruction-bound at IPC ~2.5, not latency-bound).
- perf/seq: run-time BMI2 dispatch for the decoder body (x86-64, GCC/
  clang): variable shifts cost 1 uop instead of 3; Silesia cycles/byte
  L15 7.97 -> 7.65, L2 8.73 -> 8.32. Tried and rejected: the same for the
  Huffman literal decoder (no change) and single-window field extraction
  in the sequence reader (~1%, not worth duplicating the reader).
- fixed: plane_encode leaked the Huffman candidate buffer when the LZ
  candidate won (found by ASAN on AIT G at level 1).
- api: LZ6_compress_fast_window() and LZ6_compress_HC_window() cap every
  match distance to (1 << windowLog) - 1, windowLog 10..24. The block
  format is unchanged; a decoder that keeps only the last 2^windowLog
  bytes can decode the output (e.g. a ZPAQL post-processor that must
  reserve its whole window per thread). The existing entry points are
  byte-identical to before (verified on dickens/samba/x-ray, fast and HC
  1/9/15). Measured cost on dickens HC -15: 2^22 +1.2%, 2^20 +5.5%.
- perf/seq decode: literals decoded in bulk before the sequence loop
  (two-symbol Huffman lookups, 4 lookups per 64-bit window), branch-free
  2-byte rANS renorm on budgeted unchecked blocks, 4-byte {f,off} table
  entries (L1-resident), 64-bit bit readers, two-stage sequence pipeline
  with match-source prefetch 16 sequences ahead, 16B wild copies with an
  exact tail, byte-sized offset top-8 arrays, single-pass plane
  interleave. Format unchanged; old streams decode.
  Measured (mem-to-mem, one core, interleaved vs master): Silesia L2
  ~259 -> ~374 MB/s, L15 ~271 -> ~390 MB/s; AIT L2 312 -> 398, L15
  259 -> 339.
- encode: rANS table precision capped at 12 bits (was 14/16 for larger
  alphabets) so every decode table takes the cache-resident layout.
  Output changes, format does not: Silesia L15 +0.011%, L2 +0.015%;
  AIT L15 +0.031%, L2 +0.036%.
- hardening: byte-plane blocks whose size is not a multiple of the
  stride are rejected (the tail used to be reported as decoded without
  being written; the encoder never emits them).

v1.6.3-pre
- perf/seq: the per-sequence repcode (previously a fixed 2-bit field on
  every sequence) is now folded into the offset FSE alphabet, so the
  model codes the joint distribution of (offset bucket, repcode) instead
  of paying 2 bits per sequence regardless of use. The field's real
  entropy is ~0.6 bits on text (only 12% of sequences use a repcode) and
  ~1.6-2.0 on binary. One less bitfield read per sequence, so decode gets
  faster as well.
  BREAKS THE SEQ BLOCK FORMAT: .lz6 files written by older seq levels no
  longer decode (lab format, no compatibility shim). The frame HC levels
  (--hc) are untouched and stay byte-identical.
  Measured (single block, level 15): Silesia tar 62,437,010 -> 58,693,799
  (-6.0%), level 2 76,742,753 -> 73,509,428 (-4.2%), AIT 5,367,989 ->
  5,194,253 (-3.2%); x-ray -15.1%, webster -7.6%, dickens -8.4%. Decode
  on the tar goes 149.5 -> 158.9 MB/s (L15).
- bakeoff: compat_LZ6S1_C.lz6s1 regenerated with the current block format
  (bakeoff/mk_legacy_fixture.c rebuilds it); the decode-only LZ6S1
  envelope shim is unchanged and verified against it.

v1.6.2-pre
- fixed: heap-buffer-overflow in the content-segmented loop when a
  segment landed exactly on blockSize (-B1/-B2 crashed or corrupted
  the heap; found by CLI audit + ASAN)
- fixed: truncated output after a size-triggered flush (only one block
  written; -B3/-B4 decoded to partial data with exit 0)
- fixed: legacy LZ6S1 fixture used the pre-9a177e4 block format; the
  compat shim failed on it (Error 46) — fixture regenerated, shim verified
- verified: all block sizes -B1..-B7 round-trip with seq and HC levels
  (8-file AIT matrix, incompressible and 200MB+ inputs; the small-block
  dst bound is covered by LZ6F_compressFrameBound(blockSize))

v1.6.1-pre
- seq is now the compression engine of levels 1-15 inside the standard
  .lz6 frame (the --seq side mode is gone); new magic-less per-block
  codec dispatch (LZ6S2: seq / light-LZ / raw escape in one frame)
- LZ6S3: content-segmented blocks — the CLI cuts blocks at entropy
  profile shifts so each region gets its own literal mode and transform
- fixed: plane transform skipped the normal pass on text (the lane-skew
  gate passed on novels) — dickens 57.16% -> 32.95%, reymont to 24.20%
- fixed: light-LZ fallback clobbered the seq payload when it lost
- seq block default size B7 (single block up to 256MB); fixed blockwalk
  diagnostic; MinGW binary-mode (pipes) fix; LZ6_SEG_MINSEG/THRESHOLD knobs
- decode: streaming literals, dual-layout FSE tables, order-1 combined
  entries, 16B inlined copies — Silesia L15 ~120 -> ~200 MB/s
- encode: rep-stack MTF-3 in all HC strategies; 32MB windows; hash
  widening by density (chain strategies); 32-bit __uint128_t fallback
- Silesia.tar CLI: L2 42.81% -> 36.21%, L15 29.46% -> 28.34%; AIT
  A-H L2 5,597,278 -> 5,497,008, L15 unchanged

